component SigilComparisonScope {
  goal {
    Focus native Design and Implementation comparison on explicit source
    membership so external workflows can remove temporary scope assembly.
  }

  interface {
    ScopeInput {
      Accept a versioned scope containing ordered workspace-relative Design
      root paths and the existing language-neutral Implementation selection.
      Design roots name authored Sigil files in the validated frontend bundle.
      Reject unknown fields, duplicate roots, missing roots, paths outside the
      workspace, and accidental empty selection. Intentional emptiness is
      explicit for each side and visible in the result.
      Without an explicit scope, retain full frontend Design membership and
      ordinary Implementation selection. Reject conflicting selection inputs.
    }

    FocusOrder {
      Preserve the caller's explicit root order as focus priority. Do not sort
      it away during normalization or replace it with filesystem discovery order.
      Return that order from scoped commands so the frontend and external loop
      can see what to address first and next without a temporary priority table.
      Report dependency additions deterministically after the requested roots,
      retaining their inclusion reasons without displacing explicit priorities.
      Identify requested order separately from unordered effective membership.
      Reprioritization changes order identity, not unchanged semantic bindings.
      Order guides external work; sigilc does not schedule workers, execute coding
      tasks or infer that an earlier entry has been completed.
    }

    DesignMembership {
      Resolve Design roots through frontend imports and structural ownership to
      a closed set of physical files. Include transitive import targets and
      owner declarations required by selected expansions and Concepts. Terminate
      cycles with a visited set. Preserve every authored unit in each included
      file with its physical source and canonical owner.
      Report requested roots separately from files included by dependency
      closure. Included dependency files participate in Design compilation and
      catalog construction, rather than supplying hidden comparison assumptions.
      Do not silently drop unresolved references or errors. When unresolved
      imports require conservative full-bundle membership, report that widening.
      Preserve applicable configuration, glossary context and global diagnostics.
      Do not infer source membership from temporary prose or task metadata.
    }

    ScopedComparison {
      Expose scope inspection without requiring reconstructed projections or a
      current catalog. Return current input identities and retained frontend
      diagnostics; inspection success is not a completed semantic gate.
      Use the same resolved scope for freshness inspection, preparation,
      ingestion, Design compilation, catalog export and Implementation comparison.
      Require per-source operations to target a member of their selected side.
      Report deterministic requested and effective membership and its fingerprint
      alongside current input identities and the ordinary native named states.
      Keep excluded cached objects reusable. Distinguish outside-scope objects
      from deleted sources; neither contributes assertions to the selected world.
    }
  }
}

expand SigilComparisonScope {
  constraints {
    SemanticIdentity {
      Scope membership affects assembled worlds and reports. A scope name,
      filename or external requirement ID is not a per-source semantic input.
      Reuse a projection when its existing complete semantic binding is unchanged.
      A changed effective Design identity catalog still invalidates Implementation
      projections under the existing full-catalog binding policy.
      Scoping never gives Implementation workers Design prose or relationships;
      their inputs remain captured source, fixed ontology and identity catalog.
    }

    WorkflowBoundary {
      A focused comparison reports only its effective source membership.
      It does not establish delivery of the whole refactor, test completion or
      code removal. Keep final whole-scope delivery checks external.
      Implement scope resolution in sigilc. Do not preserve the TypeScript
      compiler package, introduce a wrapper or migrate temporary task tables
      into a compiler-owned planning framework.
    }
  }

  cases {
    DependencyClosure {
      Selecting a file that imports another includes both and reports why the
      second file is present. Import cycles terminate. Selecting an expansion
      preserves its units and includes its owner's declaration file.
      An unrelated excluded file contributes no assertions or obligations.
      An unresolved import cannot manufacture a smaller successful world.
    }

    ScopeChanges {
      Reordering the same roots changes reported focus order and its identity,
      while preserving membership identity. Adding a source
      changes world membership without invalidating unchanged per-source inputs.
      Removing a file from scope does not delete it or its cached projection.
      Narrowing Design enough to change catalog identity invalidates dependent
      Implementation projections instead of reusing a previous comparison.
    }

    ObservedPriority {
      In the refactor loop, scope implementation had to be placed before export
      extraction and legacy deletion. Making that order explicit in the temporary
      Todo list exposed a requirement through real use: unordered membership
      could name the same work but could not communicate what comes first.
      Treat this as learning by observation: a necessary fact supplied by the
      temporary loop becomes a native requirement, then is implemented, exercised
      on the original task and adopted before duplicate bookkeeping is removed.
      This observation motivates FocusOrder; use native output on subsequent
      work and retire duplicate focus-priority bookkeeping once it supplies the
      same information. Preserve equivalent caller-defined
      priority in native scope output so a resumed external loop can recover it.
      Selecting roots A then B reports A first. Reprioritizing B then A reports
      B first without invalidating otherwise identical per-source projections.
      Repeated runs preserve order even when frontend or filesystem enumeration
      changes. The external workflow still establishes which work is complete.

      Focus order is a priority and reporting order, not a demand to serialize
      independent reconstructions. After one stale report, a coding agent may
      prepare and spawn bounded concurrent subagents for independent nonfresh
      sources in one released item. Native publication stays authoritative and
      the next ordered item still waits for the predecessor's full terminal
      gate. This was observed when serial source turns dominated a real
      reconstruction cycle even though native commands were fast.
    }

    ScopeLimits {
      A typo in a root fails instead of producing an empty green result.
      A successful focused comparison is not reported as complete-refactor
      convergence. Gate exits retain their subcommand-specific named states.
    }
  }
}

component SigilScopedRequest {
  goal {
    Persist one ordered implementation request and its evidence-backed
    lifecycle so the external convergence loop can retire duplicate delivery
    queue state without moving worker orchestration into sigilc.
  }

  interface {
    RequestDefinition {
      Accept a versioned request identity and a nonempty ordered list of named
      scope items. A request and each item may carry one bounded human-readable
      goal that explains intent without affecting release or semantic authority.
      Each item contains an existing ScopeInput and may name explicit predecessor
      item IDs. Preserve the immutable request definition,
      requested order, effective membership and both scope fingerprints. Reject
      duplicate IDs, unknown predecessors, cycles, duplicate scope roots and
      conflicting selectors. Order is priority among released items; it is not
      an implicit dependency. A request that wants items two and three held
      until item one finishes must name item one as their predecessor.
    }

    RequestLifecycle {
      Keep request state in durable generated workflow state separate from the
      disposable .sigil/worlds projection index. A new item is `ready` when all
      predecessors are terminal and `queued` otherwise. Re-evaluate each
      released item against the same scoped stale and gate inputs. Record the
      native named terminal state `Closed` or `Converged` only when the selected
      Design is valid, every selected projection is fresh, and the current
      scoped Implementation gate returns exit zero. Preserve `Drift` for exit
      one and `unavailable` for exit three without converting either to a
      successful completion. A changed source, catalog, scope fingerprint or
      stale projection reopens a prior terminal item with its current reason.
      Persist transitions atomically and retain the current native report
      identity and external delivery/check evidence references.
    }

    RequestInspection {
      Expose create, status, terminal completion-record and advance-equivalent
      native operations that can
      recover the request, current item states, released order, predecessor
      reasons, scope/input identities, native gate names and supporting evidence
      after a process or session restart. Status may update derived readiness,
      but no command may accept a hand-written `done` flag or infer completion
      from order, a task title, a model claim, a fixture or an empty world.
      External callers still choose workers, run coding and ordinary checks,
      and decide when to invoke the commands; sigilc only validates and records
      the deterministic state transition.

      A completion record is accepted only after every item is natively Closed
      or Converged. It retains bounded opaque references to native reports,
      projection artifacts, delivery, deletion, checks, warnings, explicit user
      overrides and bounded human-readable outcome summaries together with the
      request fingerprint and terminal gate
      snapshots. It is not a hand-written done flag: a status refresh clears it
      whenever inputs or the native lifecycle reopen any item.

      Creating a request retains its bounded structural frontend capture under
      the generated workflow record. Default inspection reuses that immutable
      capture after disposable run artifacts are removed; an explicit newer
      frontend input may reopen the lifecycle. Do not make restart recovery
      depend on a temporary tracker or caller-owned export path.
    }

    RequestArchive {
      Archive the current immutable native request under its definition
      fingerprint before a different request replaces the single active slot.
      Preserve the full request, external evidence references and terminal
      states in generated workflow history. Do not require hand-editing,
      deleting, or copying request state into a temporary tracker.
    }
  }
}

expand SigilScopedRequest {
  constraints {
    RequestBoundary {
      This is a bounded scope-request ledger, not a generic task ontology,
      worker registry, job database, daemon, scheduler, retry loop, receipt
      system or compatibility adapter. Goals and outcomes are bounded context,
      not commands, semantic facts, or completion authority. Preparation jobs remain immutable
      source descriptors and projection publication remains disposable.
      Request state never enters Design or Implementation assertions and never
      changes world authority. External delivery, tests and product approval
      remain separately evidenced, while their references may be attached to
      the request for parity inspection.
    }
  }

  cases {
    OrderedRelease {
      A request with items one, two and three may place one first in order and
      list one as the predecessor of both two and three. Initially one is
      `ready` and two and three are `queued`. A current scoped `Closed` or
      `Converged` result for one atomically releases two and three in their
      requested order. A `Drift`, unavailable comparison or stale projection
      leaves both queued and preserves the named reason.
    }

    ScopeIdentityChange {
      Reordering released items changes priority but not unchanged per-source
      bindings. Changing effective membership, Design catalog identity,
      Implementation selection or captured source bytes invalidates affected
      terminal state and prevents dependent release until a fresh current gate
      is observed. Restarting the process recovers the same request and states.
    }

    EvidenceParity {
      A status report identifies the exact scope definition, order and
      membership fingerprints, current source/projection identities, native
      Design and Implementation states, warnings or unavailable reasons, and
      external check/deletion evidence references. It never claims that a
      semantic gate alone proves code delivery or removal.
    }
  }
}
